---
/**
 * Pest library — German cockroach, Blattella germanica.
 *
 * THE ARGUMENT OF THIS PAGE: spraying is actively counterproductive on this
 * species, and the extension evidence for that is unusually strong and
 * unusually quotable. It is the one pest where the honest advice is "stop
 * buying the thing in the supermarket aisle".
 *
 * VERIFIED (AgriLife ENTO-037, texasinsects.tamu.edu, Texas A&M School IPM,
 * Texas A&M citybugs, Penn State Extension, NC State Extension, Illinois
 * Extension, Wada-Katsumata/Silverman/Schal Science 2013):
 *  - Adults ½–⅝ in, light brown, TWO DARK STRIPES on the pronotum. Wings reach
 *    the end of the abdomen "but they do not fly."
 *  - Nymphs: wing pads, two dark stripes down thorax and abdomen; a pale
 *    stripe lengthwise down the middle of the darker body.
 *  - ⚠ THE LOOKALIKE TEST IS BEHAVIOURAL AND IT MATTERS COMMERCIALLY. Asian
 *    cockroach (B. asahinai) and field cockroach (B. vaga) "look very similar"
 *    but occur OUTDOORS, whereas "German cockroaches remain strictly indoor
 *    pests in Texas". The field cockroach "prefers outdoors and is more active
 *    during daylight". An outdoor, dusk-flying, light-attracted striped roach
 *    is NOT German — and a German protocol will not fix it.
 *  - Brownbanded cockroach: pale bands across the wings, NO pronotal stripes.
 *  - Ootheca ~5/16 in, 30–40 eggs (Penn State: up to 48, usually 30–48),
 *    hatching 2–4 days after being deposited. Females produce 4–8 capsules in
 *    a lifetime. Penn State: "In one year over 10,000 descendants can be
 *    produced".
 *  - ⚠ DEVELOPMENT TIME — sources genuinely differ. ENTO-037: 50–60 days
 *    nymph to adult at average indoor temperatures, a generation in ~100 days.
 *    texasinsects: 6–7 instars over 74–85 days, up to four generations a year.
 *    Penn State: 6–31 weeks. School IPM: 3–6 generations per year. PUBLISH A
 *    RANGE and note temperature drives it.
 *  - Adult female lifespan 20–30 weeks (Penn State).
 *  - Spend "about 75 percent of its life in narrow cracks and crevices, from
 *    1/32 to 3/16 inch wide."
 *  - "Live exclusively indoors and spread only through human transport"
 *    (School IPM). An infestation ARRIVED in something.
 *  - Health: "transport germs and are associated with allergies and asthma";
 *    NC State: faeces, shed exoskeletons and saliva contain multiple
 *    allergens; "one of the leading causes of asthma in young children".
 *  - citybugs on an intervention study: baited homes 0% infestation vs 20%
 *    untreated; children in treated homes had "47 fewer days a year with
 *    asthma symptoms" and significantly fewer doctor visits.
 *    ⚠ Do NOT attribute specific figures to NEJM/Rosenstreich — NOT VERIFIED.
 *    Cite these as AgriLife's own summary, which is what they are.
 *
 * WHY SPRAYING FAILS — all quotable, all extension-sourced:
 *  - ENTO-037: "Pyrethroid insecticides are highly repellent to German
 *    cockroaches." Roaches avoid treated areas rather than dying.
 *  - School IPM: liquid residuals "can contaminate baits, rendering them
 *    ineffective through repellent action." SPRAYING NEAR BAIT SABOTAGES BAIT.
 *  - NC State: sprays and aerosols "do not provide long-term control and may
 *    even make the problem worse by repelling and dispersing cockroaches."
 *    ⚠ Use NC State's statement. A Purdue dissertation on dispersal exists but
 *    its figures were NOT VERIFIED — invent no percentages.
 *  - ENTO-037: total-release aerosols "generally do not eliminate cockroaches
 *    because they do not penetrate the cracks where most cockroaches hide";
 *    sprays "are not nearly as effective as baits".
 *  - NC State School IPM, reporting a BMC Public Health study: foggers can
 *    raise household pesticide levels by as much as 600-fold (median 85-fold).
 *  - RESISTANCE: NC State — resistance has developed to "every single class of
 *    insecticides used for their control since the 1950's". A University of
 *    Kentucky test limiting exposure to 30 minutes of direct contact found
 *    "mortality of the wild German cockroach population was below 20% for all
 *    treatments tested" across four consumer products.
 *  - WHY BAITS WORK: coprophagy enables "secondary, or horizontal, transfer"
 *    to roaches that never touched the bait. Sprays have no equivalent.
 *  - GLUCOSE AVERSION (Science 2013): in averse roaches glucose "triggers the
 *    bitter receptor neurons". It is a heritable GAIN of function. Practical:
 *    a gel being ignored while the population persists means ROTATE THE BAIT
 *    MATRIX, not switch to spray.
 *  - SANITATION MECHANISM, and it is AgriLife's own causal argument:
 *    "When food or water is scarce, cockroaches must search farther each night
 *    to survive", which "puts stress on cockroach populations and increases
 *    the effectiveness of baits and other insecticides."
 *  - School IPM's governing rule: chemical options including baits "should NOT
 *    be used on a routine or calendar-based schedule but only where cockroach
 *    presence has been confirmed."
 *  - Monitoring: "The number one monitoring tool for cockroaches is an
 *    adhesive-coated, cardboard insect monitoring trap."
 *  - Vacuums "should have a HEPA filter" because harbourages contain allergens.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import AnswerBox from '../../components/AnswerBox.astro';
import Crumbs from '../../components/Crumbs.astro';
import Faq from '../../components/Faq.astro';
import Cta from '../../components/Cta.astro';

const crumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
  { name: 'Pest library', path: '/pest-library/' },
  { name: 'German cockroach', path: '/pest-library/german-cockroach/' },
];

const quickAnswer =
  'The German cockroach is the one where the supermarket aisle actively makes things worse. Pyrethroid sprays are highly repellent to this species, so they avoid treated surfaces and disperse instead of dying, and foggers do not reach the cracks where they live. Baits and sanitation are what works, and the reason is in the biology.';

const faqs = [
