  },
  {
    q: 'Do bug bombs work?',
    a: 'No, and there is a specific reason beyond the repellency. AgriLife states total-release aerosols generally do not eliminate cockroaches because they do not penetrate the cracks where most cockroaches hide — and this species spends around three-quarters of its life in cracks between a thirty-second and three-sixteenths of an inch wide. A study reported by NC State found foggers can raise household pesticide levels by as much as 600-fold, with a median of 85-fold, while failing to reach the roaches. You end up with more pesticide in the home and the same population in the walls.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why do baits work when sprays do not?',
    a: 'Because of a habit the roaches have that sprays cannot exploit. Cockroaches practise coprophagy — they feed on the droppings of other cockroaches — which produces what researchers call secondary or horizontal transfer: the active ingredient moves from a roach that ate the bait to roaches that never went near it, including nymphs deep in harbourage that never leave. A spray kills what it touches. A bait reaches the part of the population you cannot see, which is most of it. AgriLife describes baits as the most effective insecticides for controlling cockroaches in homes.',
  },
  {
    q: 'I put down gel bait and they ignored it.',
    a: 'That is a recognised phenomenon with a genuinely interesting cause, and the fix is not to reach for a spray. Some German cockroach populations have evolved glucose aversion: in these roaches, glucose triggers the bitter receptor neurons — the ones that would normally respond to something like caffeine — so a sugar-based bait literally tastes bitter and they back away from it. It is heritable and it is a gain of function rather than a loss. The practical response is to rotate to a bait with a different matrix, often protein-based, rather than concluding baits do not work.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Are they resistant to insecticides?',
    a: 'Extensively. NC State Extension records that German cockroach populations have developed resistance to every single class of insecticide used against them since the 1950s. A University of Kentucky test that limited exposure to thirty minutes of direct contact with four common consumer products found mortality in a wild population below 20 percent for every treatment tested. That is the honest context for a product that promises to kill on contact — it may well kill the ones you spray directly and do very little to the population.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does cleaning really make that much difference?',
    a: 'Yes, and AgriLife gives the causal mechanism rather than just the instruction, which is what makes it convincing. When food or water is scarce, cockroaches must search farther each night to survive — and that, in AgriLife\'s words, puts stress on cockroach populations and increases the effectiveness of baits and other insecticides. Sanitation is not only about removing what they eat. It is about making the bait the most attractive thing in the room and forcing the roaches to travel to find it. A spotless kitchen makes a bait programme dramatically better.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Where did they come from? My house is clean.',
    a: 'They were carried in, and this is not a comment on your housekeeping. Texas A&M states that German cockroaches live exclusively indoors and spread only through human transport. In practice that means groceries, cardboard boxes, a second-hand appliance, used furniture, a delivery, a suitcase, or — in multifamily housing — movement from an adjoining unit through shared plumbing and wall voids. In a semi-arid climate like ours, where outdoor cockroach pressure is low, that is even more strictly true than elsewhere.',
  },
  {
    q: 'I found a striped roach outside. Is that a German cockroach?',
    a: 'Almost certainly not, and the distinction matters because it changes the whole approach. Texas has two very similar-looking relatives — the Asian cockroach and the field cockroach — that live outdoors, and the field cockroach is more active during daylight. German cockroaches remain strictly indoor pests in Texas. So a striped roach flying to a porch light at dusk, or found in the lawn, is one of the outdoor species. Treating that with a kitchen protocol for German cockroaches will not work, because the problem is not indoors and never was.',
  },
